Events: SDD

Purpose:
  Define the runtime event architecture for the .sdd system. Events
  are the historical record of every state change, action, policy
  decision, and system occurrence. Events are append-only and MUST
  NEVER be modified or deleted.

Owns: ./

Scope:
  Event format and envelope
  Event types and categories
  Event fields and metadata
  Event storage convention
  Event append rules
  Event bus architecture
  Event versioning
  Event producer/consumer model
  Duplicate event handling
  Dead letter queue
  Ordering guarantees
  Correlation and causation IDs
  Domain events vs system events
  Event retention and archival

Principles:
  Events are immutable — history is append-only.
  Events are the source of truth for audit and debugging.
  State can be reconstructed from events.
  Events are classified and redacted for security.
  Event ordering is guaranteed per execution_id.

EventEnvelope:
  structure:
    event:
      id: string (unique event ID)
      type: string (event type)
      version: string (event schema version)
      timestamp: ISO 8601 with timezone
      correlation_id: string (links related events)
      causation_id: string (parent event that caused this)
      actor: string (who/what caused the event)
      source: string (system component that produced event)
      payload: object (event-specific data)

  rule: every event MUST follow this envelope
  rule: event ID is unique across all events

EventTypes:
  STATE:
    description: task or execution state changed
    example: ExecutionStarted, ExecutionCompleted

  STAGE:
    description: task stage changed
    example: StageChanged AR -> BDD

  GATE:
    description: gate pass/fail result
    example: SecurityGatePassed, TestGateFailed

  ACTION:
    description: agent action taken
    example: FileWritten, TestExecuted

  APPROVAL:
    description: approval requested, granted, or rejected
    example: ApprovalRequested, ApprovalGranted

  POLICY:
    description: policy evaluated
    example: PolicyEvaluated, PolicyDenied

  TOOL:
    description: tool called or completed
    example: ToolCalled, ToolCompleted, ToolFailed

  CHECKPOINT:
    description: checkpoint created
    example: CheckpointCreated

  ERROR:
    description: error occurred
    example: ToolTimeout, NetworkError

  METRIC:
    description: metric recorded
    example: TokenUsageRecorded, CostRecorded

EventCategories:
  DomainEvents:
    description: business-level events
    examples:
      - UserCreated
      - PaymentCompleted
      - OrderCancelled
      - RequirementAdded

  SystemEvents:
    description: execution and system-level events
    examples:
      - ExecutionStarted
      - ToolCalled
      - ToolFailed
      - AgentAssigned
      - PolicyDenied
      - CheckpointCreated

EventFormat:
  minimal_event:
    2026-08-26T07:35:00+04:00 STATE EXEC-500 agent ExecutionStarted

  full_event:
    event:
      id: EVT-100
      type: STATE
      version: 1.0
      timestamp: 2026-08-26T07:35:00+04:00
      correlation_id: CORR-500
      causation_id: EVT-099
      actor: AGENT-CODER-01
      source: ExecutionEngine
      payload:
        execution_id: EXEC-500
        state_from: PENDING
        state_to: RUNNING
        reason: all prerequisites met

EventStorage:
  storage_location: .sdd/runtime/events.sdd

  format:
    # Task: TASK-100
    # Execution: EXEC-500
    # State: RUNNING

    2026-08-26T07:35:00+04:00 STATE EXEC-500 agent PENDING > RUNNING
      reason: all prerequisites met
      actor: ExecutionEngine

    2026-08-26T07:36:00+04:00 TOOL EXEC-500 agent ToolCalled
      tool: read_file
      resource: auth/login.go
      actor: AGENT-CODER-01

  rule: events are stored in a single append-only structure
  rule: events are organized by task/execution

EventRetention:
  rule: events are retained indefinitely for audit
  rule: events MAY be archived to cold storage
  rule: archive MUST remain queryable
  rule: events MUST NOT be deleted or compacted

EventVersioning:
  rule: event schemas are versioned
  example:
    ToolCalled.v1
    ToolCalled.v2

  rule: consumers MUST handle old event versions
  rule: schema changes require migration

EventProducer:
  examples:
    ExecutionEngine: ExecutionStarted, ExecutionCompleted
    ToolRunner: ToolCalled, ToolCompleted, ToolFailed
    PolicyEngine: PolicyEvaluated, PolicyDenied
    AgentRuntime: ActionProposed

EventConsumer:
  examples:
    ObservabilityService: metrics, traces
    AuditService: audit log
    NotificationService: alerts
    MetricsService: aggregations

EventBus:
  purpose: decouple producers from consumers

  properties:
    ordering: per execution_id
    delivery: at-least-once
    retry: with exponential backoff
    dead-letter: DLQ for permanently failed events
    idempotency: deduplication by event_id
    partitioning: by execution_id
    retention: configurable per event type

DuplicateEvents:
  rule: consumers MUST handle duplicate events
  rule: deduplication is by event_id
  rule: processing the same event twice MUST NOT cause side effects

DeadLetterQueue:
  rule: events that cannot be processed go to DLQ
  rule: DLQ is monitored and reprocessed
  rule: DLQ events are analyzed for systemic issues

Ordering:
  rule: ordering is guaranteed per execution_id
  rule: global ordering across executions is NOT required
  rule: ordering is implemented via partitioning

CorrelationID:
  definition:
    ID that links all events in a workflow

  example:
    CORR-500 flows through:
      API -> Task -> Execution -> Agent -> Tool -> Validation

  rule: correlation_id is set at workflow start
  rule: all subsequent events inherit correlation_id

CausationID:
  definition:
    ID of the event that caused this event

  example:
    EVENT-10: ExecutionStarted
    EVENT-11: ToolCalled (caused_by: EVENT-10)
    EVENT-12: ToolCompleted (caused_by: EVENT-11)

  rule: causation_id creates causal chain
  rule: causation_id enables root cause analysis

CorrelationVsCausation:
  correlation:
    definition: events belong to same workflow
    example: CORR-500 links all events in a request

  causation:
    definition: event A caused event B
    example: ToolCalled caused ToolCompleted

Security:
  rule: sensitive data MUST be redacted in events
  rule: SECRET classified events are never logged in plain text
  rule: event access is controlled by policy

DataClassification:
  levels:
    PUBLIC: no restrictions
    INTERNAL: internal use only
    CONFIDENTIAL: restricted access
    SECRET: highly sensitive, never in plain text

  rule: all event payloads MUST be classified
  rule: SECRET data is replaced with [REDACTED]

EventImmutability:
  rule: events cannot be modified after creation
  rule: corrections create new events, not modifications
  example:
    TOOL_FAILED
    TOOL_RETRIED
    TOOL_COMPLETED

  rule: event history is never rewritten

AIReadRestriction:
  rule: AI MUST NOT read the entire events file
  rule: AI MUST filter by task ID or time range
  rule: AI MUST only read events relevant to current task

Navigation:
  Runtime: @INDEX.sdd
  Execution: @execution.sdd
  Context: @context.sdd
  Orchestrator: @../orchestrator/INDEX.sdd
  Workflow: @../workflow/INDEX.sdd
  Protocol: @../protocol/INDEX.sdd

Rules:
  [E1] Every state change MUST produce a STATE event.
  [E2] Every stage change MUST produce a STAGE event.
  [E3] Every gate result MUST produce a GATE event.
  [E4] Every policy evaluation MUST produce a POLICY event.
  [E5] Every tool call MUST produce a TOOL event.
  [E6] Every approval request MUST produce an APPROVAL event.
  [E7] Every checkpoint MUST produce a CHECKPOINT event.
  [E8] Every error MUST produce an ERROR event.
  [E9] Every metric MUST produce a METRIC event.
  [E10] Events are immutable and append-only.
  [E11] Event ordering is guaranteed per execution_id.
  [E12] Event consumers MUST be idempotent.
  [E13] Duplicate events MUST be handled gracefully.
  [E14] Failed events go to Dead Letter Queue.
  [E15] Sensitive data MUST be redacted in all events.
  [E16] Events MUST be classified (PUBLIC, INTERNAL, CONFIDENTIAL, SECRET).
  [E17] AI MUST NOT read the entire events file — only relevant events.
  [E18] Event schemas are versioned; consumers handle old versions.
  [E19] Correlation ID links workflow events; Causation ID links causal chain.
  [E20] Events are retained indefinitely; archive remains queryable.
  [E21] Agent MUST NOT add signatures or sign-offs to event outputs.
  [E22] Agent MUST NOT use emojis, icons, or decorative symbols in event output headers or titles — reserved for human use only.

State: +
